course: dictionary
lesson: 15817
title: Words with no strings attached
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unconditionally has parts you may already know. Un- means not, and conditional means depending on an «if». So unconditionally means completely, with no conditions, limits or «if»s attached. «Mum loves me unconditionally, even on the days I am grumpy.» Her love does not depend on anything I do.
explain: Use the context clue. A conditional love would sound like «I will love you if you tidy your room». Unconditional love has no such «if». Swap your meaning into the sentence: «Mum loves me completely, with no ifs, even on the days I am grumpy» still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: The same word appears in other places. In history, «the army had to surrender unconditionally», which means giving up completely, with no terms or deals. In maths, a rule that holds unconditionally holds in every case, with no exceptions.
fact: Unconditionally is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done or held.
fact: Unconditionally means completely, with no conditions, limits or «if»s attached.
fact: To love someone unconditionally means to love them whatever they do or however they are.
fact: «Unconditional» is the adjective form, as in «unconditional love».
fact: In history, an army may «surrender unconditionally», giving up with no terms at all.
exercise: «Mum loves me unconditionally, even when I spill juice.» What does unconditionally mean here? | Only when I am being good | Very quietly | *With no ifs or limits, whatever I do | Sometimes, when she feels like it
exercise: «The army had to surrender unconditionally.» What does unconditionally suggest? | They asked for a long rest and some lunch | *They gave up completely, with no terms or deals | They fought until the sun went down
exercise: «This maths rule holds unconditionally.» What does that mean? | It works only on Tuesdays | *It works in every case, with no exceptions | It is written in capital letters | It is very hard to understand
test: «Grandma welcomes us unconditionally.» Which word means nearly the same as unconditionally? | Sometimes | *Completely | Lightly | Quietly
test: Why is unconditional love different from love with conditions? | *Because it does not depend on any «if» | Because it costs money | Because it is only for grown-ups | Because it is very loud
exam: «Christians believe God loves people unconditionally.» What does unconditionally tell us about God's love? | *It has no conditions or limits | It is only for people who are good | It changes every week | It stops when people are sad
